# `pluss::subsp::stats::ts::plot_pacf`

Draws the partial autocorrelation plot of a series into a `pluss::pl::Studio` figure.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::pl::Studio& ts::plot::pacf(pluss::pl::Studio& studio, const pluss::Series& x, const ts::plot::CorrPlotOptions& options = {}, pluss::codem::Config cfg = {});
```

## What It Does

Adds stems for the partial autocorrelation at each lag, the two lines of the white-noise band (`+CI`, `−CI`) and a zero line, with the axes labelled `lag` and the function's name. Bars outside the band point to structure. The figure is returned for chaining and can then be saved with `save_svg`, `save_pdf` or `save_png`.

## Parameters

`pluss::pl::Studio& studio`
: The figure to draw into.

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The series (a vector), at least two observations.

`const ts::plot::CorrPlotOptions& options`
: Lags, estimator and band; see [`CorrPlotOptions`](CorrPlotOptions.md).

`pluss::codem::Config cfg`
: Output configuration of the payload arrays.

## Returns

The same `Studio`, for chaining.

## Errors

- A series shorter than two observations is rejected.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Draw the partial autocorrelation plot of an autoregressive series and save it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

#include <pluss/pl/studio.h>

namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
void show(const char* label, const Series& s) {
  std::cout << std::left << std::setw(14) << label << std::right << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  for (double v : s.to_vec<double>()) std::cout << std::setw(9) << v;
  std::cout << "\n";
}
std::vector<double> noise(int n) {
  std::vector<double> e(n);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    double u = std::sin(12.9898 * (i + 1.0) + 78.233) * 43758.5453;
    u -= std::floor(u);
    e[i] = u - 0.5;
  }
  return e;
}
Series ar1(int n, double phi) {
  const auto e = noise(n);
  std::vector<double> x(n);
  double carry = 0.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    carry = phi * carry + e[i];
    x[i] = carry;
  }
  return pluss::make_series(x, {n}, f64);
}
Series walk(int n) {
  const auto e = noise(n);
  std::vector<double> x(n);
  double sum = 0.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    sum += e[i];
    x[i] = sum;
  }
  return pluss::make_series(x, {n}, f64);
}

int main() {
  const Series y = ar1(400, 0.7);
  ts::plot::CorrPlotOptions options;
  options.max_lag = 24;
  pluss::pl::Studio figure("pacf", pluss::pl::Theme::paper());
  ts::plot::pacf(figure, y, options);
  const char* dir = std::getenv("PLUSSMA_GUIDE_ASSETS");
  if (dir != nullptr) figure.save_svg(std::string(dir) + "/ts_pacf.svg");
  std::cout << "figure drawn with " << options.max_lag << " lags\n";
}
```

![The partial autocorrelation of an autoregressive series](assets/ts_pacf.svg)


